% =======================================================
\section{Adaptive Frequency Tracking}
\label{sec:freq-tracking}

The direction estimator requires a stable carrier-frequency estimate from the
wave-band inertial signal.  The reported direction results use KalmANF
\cite{Ali2023DAFx}; the implementation also compiles the alternative policies
documented in Appendix~\ref{app:tracker-alternatives}.  Using vertical specific
force or levelled vertical inertial acceleration, the selected tracker produces
a raw frequency that is clamped and smoothed to give $\bar f_k$.  The direction
stage advances its quadrature phase from this estimate but does not assume that
the tracker's absolute phase is aligned with horizontal motion.

For normalized operation the scalar tracker input is
\[
u(t)=\frac{a_z(t)}{g},\qquad
\omega=2\pi f,\qquad
\phi(t)=\int_0^t \omega(s)\,ds .
\]

\subsection{\textsc{KalmANF}: Kalman-updated adaptive notch}

KalmANF models the signal with a second-order notch parameterized by
$\chi=2\cos\omega$. It treats $\chi$ as a slowly varying scalar state and
updates it from the instantaneous prediction error.

Embed the notch with $\chi\in(-2,2)$:
\begin{align}
\vct{x}_{k+1} &= \begin{bmatrix}0&1\\-1&\chi_k\end{bmatrix}\vct{x}_k
 + \begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix}u_k,
&y_k&=\begin{bmatrix}1&0\end{bmatrix}\vct{x}_k,\\
\chi_{k+1}&=\chi_k+w_k,
&w_k&\sim\mathcal{N}(0,q_\chi).
\end{align}
For $\varepsilon_k=u_k-y_k$, the scalar correction is
\begin{align}
\widetilde\chi_k&=\chi_k,&p_{\chi,k}^-&=p_{\chi,k-1}+q_\chi,\\
\kappa_k&=\frac{p_{\chi,k}^-\,\partial\varepsilon_k/\partial\chi}
{(\partial\varepsilon_k/\partial\chi)^2p_{\chi,k}^-+r},\\
\chi_k&\leftarrow\widetilde\chi_k+\kappa_k\varepsilon_k,&
p_{\chi,k}&=(1-\kappa_k\,\partial\varepsilon_k/\partial\chi)p_{\chi,k}^-.
\end{align}
The derivative follows the standard state-sensitivity recursion, and
\[
\hat\omega_k=\arccos\!\left(\tfrac12\clip(\chi_k,-2,2)\right),
\qquad \hat f_k=\hat\omega_k/(2\pi).
\]

The frequency estimate is used as a slowly varying carrier, not as a claim that
the sea is monochromatic.  The quadrature direction estimator in
Sec.~\ref{sec:direction} estimates both sine and cosine coefficients, so a
constant carrier-phase offset does not bias the recovered propagation axis.
Frequency-tracker alternatives are retained for implementation portability but
are not compared in the direction-validation evidence reported here.
